# Flutter / Dart
.dart_tool/
.packages
build/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# pubspec.lock IS committed, deliberately — note there is no rule for it here.
#
# The Dart guidance splits on what the package is: a library ignores its
# lockfile because its consumers resolve their own, an application commits it
# because the resolved set is part of what was tested. This is an application,
# and it is one shipped to a store. Ignoring the lockfile means a fresh clone
# resolves whatever is newest that day, so the build that goes to Play is not
# the build anyone ran — and a plugin that breaks on a point release breaks in
# the release, not on the machine that could have caught it.

# Gradle. Build caches, and a file of absolute paths to this machine's SDKs.
.gradle/
android/.gradle/
android/local.properties

# IDE
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Play upload signing — never commit these.
#
# key.properties holds the keystore password in plain text, and the keystore
# itself is the one thing here that cannot be regenerated: Play requires every
# update to carry the same certificate. It lives outside this project on
# purpose — see android/key.properties.example. These rules are the second line
# of defence, for the day a copy lands inside the tree anyway.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
android/key.properties
android/keystore-credentials.txt
*.jks
*.keystore

# Build output. The bundle is ~47 MB and is rewritten on every build, so
# committing it would add that much to history each time, for a file anyone
# with the keystore can reproduce.
*.aab
*.apk

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# assets/ IS committed, deliberately — note there is no rule for it below.
#
# In the website repo those two files are generated by `npm run flutter:assets`
# and ignored, because they are reproducible in seconds. This project is
# standalone; there is no such script here. Ignoring them makes the repository
# unbuildable — pubspec declares both, and a fresh clone fails on a missing
# asset. That is not hypothetical: this folder arrived with no assets/ at all
# and could not be built until they were copied in by hand.
#
# bible.db is 7.5 MB. That is a fair price for a repository that builds.
# Refresh both from the website repo whenever the catalog changes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Derived from branding/app-icon.png by tool/make_branding.dart. The source
# picture beside them is the one to keep.
branding/adaptive-foreground.png
branding/splash-icon.png

# What `flutter test` writes beside a golden it could not match: the rendered
# image, the master, and two diffs, per failing test. They are for looking at
# while you work out which of the two is wrong, and half a megabyte of them
# appears the first time a golden drifts. The goldens themselves are committed;
# the post-mortem is not.
test/**/failures/
